---
/**
 * The three portfolios side by side, with the with-trend portfolio's version for today's
 * prices as a fourth column, a short test for which one is yours, and the ladder that
 * sets how much of the trend fund to hold.
 *
 * The comparison table types its cells because a table is where a reader scans; the
 * measured numbers in the prose beneath render from figure records.
 */
import AllocationBar from "~/components/AllocationBar.astro";
import Breakout from "~/components/Breakout.astro";
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import Ladder from "~/components/Ladder.astro";
import Verdict from "~/components/Verdict.astro";
import { PORTFOLIOS, TODAY_HOLDINGS, type Holding } from "~/content/portfolios";
import type { Confidence } from "~/lib/rungs";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

interface Column {
  readonly heading: string;
  readonly href: string;
  readonly holdings: readonly Holding[];
  readonly fee: string;
  readonly feeOn10k: string;
  readonly adds: string;
  readonly worst: string;
  readonly confidence: Confidence;
}

const withTrend = PORTFOLIOS[2];
if (withTrend === undefined) throw new Error("The with-trend portfolio is missing from PORTFOLIOS");

const COLUMNS: readonly Column[] = [
  ...PORTFOLIOS.map((p, i) => ({
    heading: `${p.number}. ${p.name}`,
    href: p.href,
    holdings: p.holdings,
    fee: p.fee,
    feeOn10k: p.feeOn10k,
    adds: [
      "Fees and tax against whatever you hold now: 0.04 to 2.70 points a year",
      "About 0.8 points a year over a cheap index mix, 1990 to 2026",
      "About 2.0 points a year over an all-stock fund, 1929 to 2025, fading to 0.4 since 2009",
    ][i] as string,
    worst: [
      "Fell 52.7% and took 63 months to recover, 1990 to 2026",
      "Cheap US stocks 54.3% behind the market over 17.7 years, still not recovered",
      "Fell 50.3% and took 42 months to recover, 1990 to 2026, with RSST at 25%",
    ][i] as string,
    confidence: p.confidence,
  })),
  {
    heading: "3, for today's prices",
    href: `${withTrend.href}#the-version-for-todays-prices`,
    holdings: TODAY_HOLDINGS,
    fee: "0.33%",
    feeOn10k: "$33",
    adds: "About 0.6 points a year less than the 30% version over 1929 to 2025; 0 to 0.2 less at today's likely returns",
    worst: "1.8 points shallower than the 30% version, 1929 to 2025",
    confidence: "Too close to call",
  },
];

const holdingsText = (holdings: readonly Holding[]) => holdings.map((h) => `${h.ticker} ${h.weight}%`).join(", ");

const ROWS = [
  { label: "Holdings", cells: COLUMNS.map((c) => holdingsText(c.holdings)) },
  { label: "Fee a year", cells: COLUMNS.map((c) => c.fee) },
  { label: "Fee on $10,000", cells: COLUMNS.map((c) => c.feeOn10k) },
  { label: "What it adds over the one before", cells: COLUMNS.map((c) => c.adds) },
  { label: "Worst stretch", cells: COLUMNS.map((c) => c.worst) },
] as const;

const LADDER = [
  { worstFall: "−30%", rsst: "11%" },
  { worstFall: "−40%", rsst: "15%", extra: "Plus 10 points in inflation-protected Treasuries (TIPS)" },
  { worstFall: "−50%", rsst: "19%", extra: "Plus 10 points in TIPS" },
  { worstFall: "−60%", rsst: "24%" },
  { worstFall: "About −70%", rsst: "30%", published: true },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="The three portfolios, side by side"
  wide
  description="Holdings, fees, what each portfolio adds, how far each has fallen, and a short test for which one is yours."
>
  <Hero
    title="Three portfolios, side by side"
    lede="Each one contains the one before it. The question is how much of the clever stuff you can hold through a bad decade."
  />

  <Breakout scroll class="mt-8" heading="The comparison" maxWidth="64rem">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base sm:min-w-[58rem]">
      <caption class="sr-only">The three portfolios and the today's-prices version compared on holdings, fees, what each adds, worst stretch and confidence</caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left align-bottom">
          <td role="cell" class="py-2 pr-4"></td>
          {
            COLUMNS.map((c) => (
              <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class="eyebrow py-2 pr-4">
                <a href={c.href} class="link">{c.heading}</a>
              </th>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-2">
        {
          ROWS.map((row) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule align-top">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-medium text-ink">
                {row.label}
              </th>
              {row.cells.map((cell, i) => (
                <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[i]?.heading} class="py-2 pr-4">
                  {cell}
                </td>
              ))}
            </tr>
          ))
        }
        <tr role="row" class="align-top">
          <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-4 text-left font-medium text-ink">How sure I am</th>
          {
            COLUMNS.map((c) => (
              <td role="cell" data-label={c.heading} class="py-2 pr-4">
                <Verdict word={c.confidence} />
              </td>
            ))
          }
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </Breakout>

  <div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-5">
    {
      COLUMNS.map((c) => (
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow mb-1">{c.heading}</p>
          <AllocationBar holdings={c.holdings} name={c.heading} />
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <Callout kind="caveat" label="Three comparisons, not one" class="mt-8">
    <p>The "what it adds" row compares each portfolio with a different thing, on purpose. Portfolio one is compared
    with whatever you hold today, because that is where its saving comes from. Portfolios two and three are
    compared with a cheap index mix holding the same split of US and international stocks. The today's-prices
    version is compared with the 30% version beside it. The figures do not add up to one number, and I have not
    added them.</p>
  </Callout>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What each is for</h2>

    <p><strong>One fund, held well</strong> is the market. Over ten years to mid-2025, 90.3% of US stock funds run
    by professionals did worse than it. The four habits that go with it, the cheapest version of the fund, the
    right account for each piece, an ETF rather than a mutual fund, and no trading, are worth between 0.04 and
    2.70 points a year depending on what you hold now, and all of it is arithmetic rather than forecast.</p>

    <p><strong>Value lean</strong> replaces the one fund with six, and leans toward companies that are cheaper,
    smaller and more profitable than average. Over 1990 to 2026 that lean earned about
    <Figure id="value-lean-gap" inline showUnit={false} /> a year over a cheap index mix, with a range of 0.4 to
    1.3.</p>

    <p><strong>With trend</strong> adds one fund, RSST, that holds US stocks and a trend-following program at the
    same time, adding an idea on top of stocks rather than swapping stocks for it. On 96 years of data it added
    about <Figure id="trend-96-year-gap" inline /> over an all-stock fund. Since 2009 the gap has been
    <Figure id="trend-gap-since-2009" inline showUnit={false} />, which is too small to tell from luck. The version
    for today's prices moves five points from RSST into TIPS, Treasury bonds whose value rises with inflation, and
    is the version I would hold now.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>Which one is yours</h2>

    <p>Answer one question: how far could your portfolio fall before you sold?</p>

    <ul>
      <li>If the answer is less than 30%, none of these as written. Hold fewer stocks and more bonds.</li>
      <li>If it is 30% to 50%, hold portfolio one with some bonds beside it, or portfolio three at the row of the
      ladder below that matches your number. The cheap mix and the trend version both fell by about half at some
      point since 1990.</li>
      <li>If it is 50% or more and you would rather match the market than risk trailing it for a decade, hold
      portfolio one.</li>
      <li>If it is 50% or more and you want a lean with a long record, hold portfolio two.</li>
      <li>If it is 50% or more and you want a real chance of beating the market, hold portfolio three, with the
      RSST share the ladder gives for your number.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The ladder</h2>

    <p>The share of portfolio three in RSST is set by the worst fall you would sit through. The published 30%
    assumes a tolerance near 70%, which is more than most people mean when they say they can hold on. The version
    for today's prices, RSST at 25% with 5 points in TIPS, sits between the −50% and −60% rows.</p>
  </div>

  <Ladder rows={LADDER} class="my-6" />

  <div class="prose">
    <p>The TIPS rows come from today's stock prices. Over the past 96 years the today's-prices version would have
    trailed the 30% version by about <Figure id="working-default-gap" inline showUnit={false} /> a year, and at
    today's more modest expected returns by 0 to 0.2 points, in exchange for a worst fall 1.8 points shallower.
    The <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">with-trend page</a> explains the choice.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>The decision that is bigger than all of this</h2>

    <p>How much of your money is in stocks at all matters more than anything on this page. Moving from 60% stocks to
    90% stocks added about <Figure id="equity-share-step" inline showUnit={false} /> a year over 1963 to 2025, and
    it takes about <Figure id="equity-step-horizon" inline /> of results to be confident that gap was not luck.
    Nothing above changes that decision. Set your stock share from the fall you can sit through, and then pick a
    portfolio.</p>

    <p>Every figure on this page is before tax and after fund costs. What the numbers are worth to you depends on
    your accounts and on what you hold today, and no page here assumes either.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
